# When does sand become a heap? The sorites paradox
tags: ["sorites paradox","vagueness","fuzzy logic","epistemicism","philosophy of language"]
teaches: ["concepts/sorites-paradox","concepts/theories-of-vagueness","concepts/fuzzy-logic"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "If one grain never makes the difference, how do heaps exist? Is the boundary sharp but unknown, blurred, or not there at all?"
next: {"title":"Would you pay a fortune for an infinitely valuable game? The St Petersburg paradox","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/10-would-you-pay-a-fortune-for-an.cast"}

## The sorites paradox
vars: {"k":1,"j":480,"n":{"expr":"max(1, round(1000000*k^3))"}}
sources:
    - id: sep_sorites
      title: Sorites Paradox (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
      authors: Dominic Hyde and Diana Raffman
      year: 2018
      finding: The puzzle is usually credited to Eubulides of Miletus (4th century BC); 'sorites' comes from Greek soros, heap; the main responses are epistemicism, supervaluation, degree theories and nihilism.
    - id: williamson1994
      title: Vagueness
      authors: Timothy Williamson
      year: 1994
      finding: 'Epistemicism: vague words have sharp boundaries, fixed by use, which we cannot know because knowledge needs a margin for error.'
    - id: zadeh1965
      title: Fuzzy sets
      authors: Lotfi A. Zadeh
      year: 1965
      finding: 'Membership in a set by degrees between 0 and 1 (Information and Control 8: 338–353).'
    - id: sendai
      title: Namboku Line (Sendai Subway)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2024
      finding: Opened 15 July 1987 with Hitachi's fuzzy-logic automatic train operation controlling acceleration, braking and stopping.
    - id: unger1979
      title: There are no ordinary things
      authors: Peter Unger
      year: 1979
      finding: 'A sorites argument that there are no heaps, stones or tables (Synthese 41: 117–154).'
    - id: philpapers2020
      title: 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey'
      authors: David Bourget and David Chalmers
      year: 2023
      finding: 'Vagueness: semantic 52%, epistemic 24%, metaphysical 21% (accept or lean towards; 1,431 answering; several answers allowed).'

