    card title "When does sand become a heap?"

A: Here's a question that sounds silly: how many grains of sand does it take to make a heap?
    draw ground heap count

B: Silly is (@highlight heap@) right. A heap is just a lot of sa (@draw b1@)nd. You know one when you see one.

A: Fine. Then t (@highlight count@)his pile of a million grains is a heap. (@draw grain@) Now I take away one grain.

    move grain to.x 560 to.y 237 duration 1

    animate k 0.9999997 duration 0.3

B: Still a heap, obviously. One grain never makes the difference.
    draw a12 b2

    erase grain

A: Then take another, and another. Each time the same reasoning works: if it was a heap a moment ago, it's a heap now.
    blocking false

    animate k 0.1 duration 6 easing linear

A: Do that nine hundred and ninety-nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine times, and you're down to a single grain.
    blocking false

    animate k 0.01 duration 3.5

    draw a23 b3

B: A single grain is (@draw x_b3@) not a heap. I'd like that on the record.

A: That's the sorites paradox, from  (@draw soros@)the Greek soros, a heap. It's usually credited to Eu (@draw eub@)bulides of Miletus, in the fourth century BC.

A: Each step looks innocent, and the conclusion is impossible. So one step must be wrong.
    highlight b1 b2

B: Not the first one: a million grains is a hea (@point at.ref b2 gesture circle@)p. So it has to be the second.

A: Most people land there. But think what rejecting it means: somewher (@draw lastheap a_last@)e there's a heap that stops being one when you take away one grain.

B: Which grain is that? I'd like to meet it.
    highlight lastheap

    erase soros eub

    erase lastheap a_last

A: That's the real question, and there are three answers: the (@draw r_sharp@) line is sharp but hidden, the li (@draw r_blur@)ne is blurred, or there's no line beca (@draw r_none@)use there are no heaps.

    clear

    card title "Sharp but hidden"

A: Put every pile on one line: one grain on the left, a million on the right. Upw (@draw g_y g_f g_t@)ards: is it a heap, false or true?
    draw g_ax g_x1 g_x2 g_xl

A: The first answer says the tr (@draw step@)uth jumps: false, false, false, then at one exact (@draw qm@) number of grains, true for good.

A: Timothy Williamson defended this in his 1994 book Vagueness. The view is called episte (@draw cantknow@)micism: the line is real, we just can't know where it is.
    draw williamson

    erase williamson

B: Who put it there? Nobody voted on grain number four thousand and something.
    blocking false

    animate j 300 duration 1.4 ghost ["step"]

    animate j 680 duration 1.8

    animate j 480 duration 1.2

    erase step_ghost

A: His answer: our use of the word fixes it, the whole pattern of how everyone says heap, too intricate for anyone to read off.
    highlight step

A: And our judgments always need a m (@draw margin margin_l@)argin for error, so we can never be sure which side of the line a pile is on.

B: So heap has an exact meaning that nobody knows. (@highlight cantknow@) That sounds like a magic trick.
    point at.ref qm gesture circle

A: Plenty of philosophers agree. Its de (@erase margin margin_l cantknow@)fenders reply that it keeps ordinary (@highlight g_t g_f@) logic whole: every pile is a heap, or it isn't.

    erase qm card_2_title card_2_line

    card title Blurred

A: The second answer drops that. Truth isn't all or nothing; it comes in  (@draw dimmer@)degrees, like a dimmer, not a switch.
    fade step to 0.2

A: Make it simple: say the tr (@draw ramp@)uth falls evenly, one millionth for every grain you take away.

B: Nobody thinks half a million grains is half a heap.
    point at.x 515 at.y 405 gesture tap

A: Fair. The r (@draw scurve scurve_l@)eal curve is flat at both ends, but the point survives: each grain changes the truth only a little.

    erase scurve scurve_l dimmer

A: So 'if this is a heap, one grain less is too' is al (@draw tick a_tick@)most true: true to all but one millionth.

B: Almost true, every single step. So a single grain ends up almost a heap?
    highlight ramp

    hide g_ax g_x1 g_x2 g_xl g_y g_f g_t step ramp tick a_tick

    ask "After all 999,999 steps, how true is '1 grain is a heap'? Set your guess." estimate.min 0 estimate.max 100 estimate.value 0 estimate.unit "% true" gap false store g default "50" voice b
        right: Right: you said {g}, and it's almost nothing.
        wrong: You said {g}; it's almost nothing.

    erase estimate_1

    show g_ax g_x1 g_x2 g_xl g_y g_f g_t step ramp tick a_tick

A: Hardly at all.  (@draw chain_box chain_line_1@)Nine hundred and ninety-nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine steps, each  (@draw chain_line_2@)losing a millionth: almost th (@draw chain_line_3@)e whole truth is gone.

B: So nearly-true steps, piled up, leak all the truth away.
    flow ramp reverse true

    erase chain

A: That's the diagnosis of fuzzy logic, which (@draw zadeh_l@) Lotfi Zadeh launched in 1965. In 1987 the Sendai subway in Ja (@draw train@)pan opened with trains that br (@draw train_l@)ake and accelerate by fuzzy rules.

B: But it swap (@highlight ramp@)s one sharp line for a million sharp numbers. Who deci (@draw p04 objection@)des a pile is exactly point four of a heap?

    clear

    card title "Is there a line at all?"

    fade step to 1 duration 0.1

    draw g_ax g_x1 g_x2 g_y g_f g_t step ramp l_sharp l_blur

A: The third answer is the boldest. Peter Unger argued i (@draw none@)n 1979 that there are no heaps at all, (@draw l_none@) nor stones, nor tables: the words have nothing to stand on.

B: So my sandcastle never existed. That's one way to win an argument.
    highlight none l_none

A: So who's winning? A  (@erase g_ax g_x1 g_x2 g_y g_f g_t step ramp none l_sharp l_blur l_none@)2020 survey asked philosophers where the  (@draw m_title@)vagueness of words like heap comes from.

    ask "Where does the blur in 'heap' come from? Pick one." poll.choices [{"text":"In what we can know","share":0.24},{"text":"In our words","share":0.52},{"text":"In the world itself","share":0.21}] poll.source philpapers2020 store p default "In our words" voice a
        right: You picked {p}, like {p.share} of the philosophers.

    erase poll_1_buttons

A: About  (@draw m_w m_w3@)half said the blur is in our words, a quarter said  (@draw m_k m_k3@)it's in what we can know, and a fifth said t (@draw m_o m_o3@)he world itself is vague.

B: So where's Williamson's hidden line on that list? And Unger's 'no heaps'?
    highlight m_k m_w m_o

A: Williamson i (@draw m_k2@)s 'what we can know'. 'Our words' is the bigges (@draw m_w2@)t camp: the word was never pinned down, so a sen (@highlight m_w m_w2@)tence is true when every sensible sharpening agrees. 'The world' means  (@draw m_o2@)vague things, like a cloud's edge. Unger isn't on the list.

B: So no answer has won. What do you do in the meantime?
    highlight m_w m_k m_o

    erase m_title m_k m_k2 m_k3 m_w m_w2 m_w3 m_o m_o2 m_o3

A: The oldest advice is from the Stoic Ch (@draw chrys@)rysippus: when someone questions you grain by grain, stop answering before the hard cases.

    erase chrys

B: The law took that a (@draw age_ax age_l age0 age30 age_kid age_ad@)dvice. It never asks when you're grown  (@draw age18 age18_l@)up; it picks your eighteenth birthday.

A: Exactly: a line drawn on purpose, where the word itself has none.
    highlight age18 age18_l

    clear

    card title "When does sand become a heap?"

A: (@draw c1 c12 c2 c23 c3 x_c3 x_c2@) So most answers break the same step, 'one grain never matters': it's f (@draw tag1@)alse at one hidden grain, or nearly true each time, wit (@draw tag2@)h the near-truths leaking away.

B: Unless, with Unger, there  (@draw tag3@)was never a heap to begin with. Either way, words li (@highlight c2 tag1 tag2@)ke heap, tall and grown-up have no line we can find.

    quiz "Pluck one hair from a full head: still not bald. Do it 100,000 times: still not bald? Most logicians blame which step?" voice a
        right: Each hair is harmless: that small step fails, either at one hidden hair or by being only nearly true, with the near-truths adding up.
        wrong: Think of the heap: which line did a million tiny steps wear down?
        * A full head isn't bald
        + Each hair is harmless
        * Chaining so many steps

    erase c1 c12 c2 c23 c3 x_c2 x_c3 tag1 tag2 tag3

    path ground2 #8f887c points [[260,230],[740,230]]
    sector heap2 #3d3833 x 500 y 230 radius 2 start 0 end 180 bind.radius 200*k style.fill #f2c14e style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.95
    text count2 "grains: {n:0,}" x 500 y 185 font_size 38

B: So when does sand become a heap? Somewhere between one grain and a million, and nobody can tell you which grain.
    blocking false

    animate k 1 duration 5

## Where next
end_page: true

