      ms: 7880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1av5x1u'
    '|a||So ''if this is a heap, one grain less is too'' is almost true: true to all but one millionth.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:19h98gg'
    '|b||Almost true, every single step. So a single grain ends up almost a heap?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5520
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:yqpnxb'
    '|b||After all 999,999 steps, how true is ''1 grain is a heap''? Set your guess.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7520
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1dk2njg'
    '|a||Hardly at all. Nine hundred and ninety-nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine steps, each losing a millionth: almost the whole truth is gone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9480
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||So nearly-true steps, piled up, leak all the truth away.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4600
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:11oslgx'
    '|a||That''s the diagnosis of fuzzy logic, which Lotfi Zadeh launched in 1965. In 1987 the Sendai subway in Japan opened with trains that brake and accelerate by fuzzy rules.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1cf1blc'
    '|b||But it swaps one sharp line for a million sharp numbers. Who decides a pile is exactly point four of a heap?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:13lkbvm'
    '|a||The third answer is the boldest. Peter Unger argued in 1979 that there are no heaps at all, nor stones, nor tables: the words have nothing to stand on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:13zsifp'
    '|b||So my sandcastle never existed. That''s one way to win an argument.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4480
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:8gmo61'
    '|a||So who''s winning? A 2020 survey asked philosophers where the vagueness of words like heap comes from.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8040
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1bxfy17'
    '|a||Where does the blur in ''heap'' come from? Pick one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3320
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:wdy3zb'
    '|a||You picked In what we can know, like 24% of the philosophers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4000
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||About half said the blur is in our words, a quarter said it''s in what we can know, and a fifth said the world itself is vague.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8840
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||So where''s Williamson''s hidden line on that list? And Unger''s ''no heaps''?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4760
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1csbrcc'
    '|a||Williamson is ''what we can know''. ''Our words'' is the biggest camp: the word was never pinned down, so a sentence is true when every sensible sharpening agrees. ''The world'' means vague things, like a cloud''s edge. Unger isn''t on the list.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 16480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:wccsc8'
    '|b||So no answer has won. What do you do in the meantime?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4120
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1g6tr2k'
    '|a||The oldest advice is from the Stoic Chrysippus: when someone questions you grain by grain, stop answering before the hard cases.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1plu0m5'
    '|b||The law took that advice. It never asks when you''re grown up; it picks your eighteenth birthday.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5520
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1bn70su'
    '|a||Exactly: a line drawn on purpose, where the word itself has none.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4840
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1xpsfyi'
    '|a||So most answers break the same step, ''one grain never matters'': it''s false at one hidden grain, or nearly true each time, with the near-truths leaking away.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:yxje29'
    '|b||Unless, with Unger, there was never a heap to begin with. Either way, words like heap, tall and grown-up have no line we can find.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8760
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1n1tr36'
    '|a||Pluck one hair from a full head: still not bald. Do it 100,000 times: still not bald? Most logicians blame which step?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:17lxrvm'
    '|a||Each hair is harmless: that small step fails, either at one hidden hair or by being only nearly true, with the near-truths adding up.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10200
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||So when does sand become a heap? Somewhere between one grain and a million, and nobody can tell you which grain.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7920
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||Next: Would you pay a fortune for an infinitely valuable game? The St Petersburg paradox':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5960
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Think of the heap: which line did a million tiny steps wear down?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5280
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
